  "query_preview": "..\nLarge upper low over the Gulf of Alaska will keep the upper flow \nover our area into tomorrow generally from the SW. Save for \ncompressional warming effects ahead of the cold front on Friday, \ntomorrow looks to be the warmest day this week with highs in the \nupper 70s and 80s. While these temperatures are much above average\nfor this time of year, the daytime temperatures will not be \nbreaking high temperature records. Record highest minimum \ntemperatures, though, may be up for grabs for some locations \ntonight and tomorrow night.\n\nA vigorous upper trough will begin to swing east through the \nPacific NW tomorrow afternoon. With a surface low over \nwestern/central Montana, southerly winds will increase throughout\nthe day. A Lake Wind Advisory will be necessary once the event is\ncloser. Late tomorrow night into early Saturday morning a surface\nlow will move NE into central Sask bringing a cold front followed\nby a wind shift and much cooler conditions. Temperatures will \ndrop back close to normal levels for next week.\n\nWesterly upper level flow persist across the region through much\nof next week. Guidance has begun to show a disturbance moving\nacross the region on Monday. This feature represents the next\npotential for widespread rain across the area. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNo changes to NBM. Low confidence in the timing and full extent of\ndense fog. Issued the Dense Fog Advisory through 10 AM, with the\nexpectation +/- 2 hours is likely.",
